If $\overline{r}$ is a non-degenerate Thurston's hyperbolic sphere packing metric on $(M,\mathcal{T},\Phi)$ with $K_{ij}(\overline{r})\sin^2\Phi_{ij}\geq0$ for any edge $\{ij\}\in E$,
then by Theorem \ref{Lem: matrix_negative}, $\mathrm{Hess}_r \mathcal{F}(\overline{r})>0$ and hence the function $\mathcal{F}$ is a strictly convex function on a convex neighborhood $U$ of $\overline{r}$.

Suppose there exist two different Thurston's hyperbolic sphere packing metrics $r_1$ and $r_2$ in $U$ such that $K_i(r_1)=K_i(r_2)$ for all $i\in V$.
Set
\begin{equation*}
f(t)=\mathcal{F}((1-t)r_1+tr_2),\ t\in [0,1].
\end{equation*}
Then $f(t)$ is a $C^1$ smooth convex function of $t\in [0,1]$ with
\begin{equation*}
f'(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{|V|}\nabla_{r_i} \mathcal{F}|_{(1-t)r_1+tr_2}\cdot (r_{2,i}-r_{1,i})
=\sum_{i=1}^{|V|}K_i|_{(1-t)r_1+tr_2}\cdot (r_{2,i}-r_{1,i}),
\end{equation*}
where $|V|$ denotes the cardinality of the vertex set.
By the assumption that $K_i(r_1)=K_i(r_2)$, we have $f'(0)=f'(1)$,
which implies $f'(t)$ is a constant by the convexity of $f(t)$.
Thus $f''(t)\equiv0$ for $t\in [0, 1]$.
Combining with
\begin{equation*}
f''(t)=(r_2-r_1)^T\cdot \mathrm{Hess}_r \mathcal{F}|_{(1-t)r_1+tr_2} \cdot (r_2-r_1)
\end{equation*}
for $t\in [0,1]$ and $\mathrm{Hess}_r \mathcal{F}>0$,
we have $r_1=r_2$.
\hfill{Q.E.D.}\smallskip

Define the following functional
\begin{equation*}
